{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774523090,"nonce":"xKxhZDxtvLzA2lDKGk+2q9kpZV8=","title":"Single-writer architecture for git-bug lock contention","message":"Currently every component (watcher, CLI, containers via outbox) independently calls git-bug through GitBugTracker._run() with lock retry and exponential backoff. Under load (multiple sessions, frequent redumps, CLI commands), retries pile up and commands stall waiting for the lock.\n\nCurrent state: Throttled redumps to 30s per session (REQ-016b / REQ-025), which reduced lock acquisitions from ~60/min to ~4/min per session. But with multiple concurrent sessions + CLI usage + outbox processing + sync, contention still occurs.\n\nProposed: single-writer architecture\n\nFunnel all git-bug operations through a single serialized writer (the watcher process):\n\n1. Writer thread in the watcher owns the git-bug lock exclusively. All git-bug calls go through a queue.\n2. CLI commands (nightshift issue, accept, reject, etc.) send requests to the watcher via a local socket or named pipe instead of calling git-bug directly.\n3. Outbox processing already goes through the watcher — no change needed.\n4. Sync/pull/push already runs in the watcher — no change needed.\n\nBenefits:\n- Zero lock contention — only one process ever touches git-bug\n- CLI commands get immediate responses instead of retry loops\n- Predictable ordering of operations\n\nConsiderations:\n- Watcher must be running for CLI to work (or fall back to direct call with retry)\n- Need a simple IPC protocol (Unix socket + JSON-lines)\n- Graceful degradation when watcher is down\n\nREQ: needs new requirement (REQ-026 or similar). Relates to REQ-025 (lock retry).","files":null}]}